            <ArticleTitle>Understandings of participation in daily activity services among people with intellectual disabilities: A pedagogical sociocultural perspective.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1744629517743578</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1744629517743578</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This study sought to explore different understandings of participation in daily activity services among people with intellectual disabilities. Using a pedagogical sociocultural perspective, the research focused on individuals' perspectives and understandings as well as their account of social interaction, working and learning. In all, 17 people working in daily activity services were interviewed once, and, of these, 14 were interviewed a second time. Using qualitative content analysis, two themes and three categories were singled out. The results show that participation can be understood in two major ways: social interaction and performing work tasks.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
